# Supplied content (fictional)

Title: Two places for the repair workshop

Station hall
Capacity: 48 people
Hire: €240 per evening
Access: Step-free entrance, next to the bus station
Constraint: Tools and tables must be removed each evening

Garden room
Capacity: 32 people
Hire: €160 per evening
Access: Step-free entrance, 700 m from the bus station
Constraint: Tools can stay in a locked cupboard

All costs are provisional quotes for one evening; staffing and insurance are excluded.

## Audience and intention
The neighbourhood committee is discussing both venues before choosing. Give both options equal consideration; neither is recommended.
---
name: kata
description: Arrange supplied content into a clear visual layout, choose a suitable existing form, or retain the current layout. Use when placement, space, grouping, or reading order needs attention. Does not author or rewrite content or infer its internal relationships.
---

# Kata

Kata helps people arrange supplied content so its intended message remains clear. Work with the content as provided: consider its placement, available space, grouping, and reading order. Do not write or rewrite prose, derive relationships, redesign the inside of a supplied chart, table, infographic, code sample, or other content, or invent material to make a layout fit.

The brief and supplied content may state an audience, question, or priority. Use these to assess layout. Do not invent a primary claim or stronger priority. If a consequential layout choice depends on missing intent, ask; otherwise preserve the supplied treatment or give equally important material equal emphasis.

## Choose an arrangement

Start with the audience and what this item should communicate. Identify the relationship the composition needs to express: for example, equal consideration or a supported lead with accompanying context. Content type alone does not decide the layout.

Before choosing a treatment, consider a meaningful alternative using the same supplied content. Compare what size, placement, grouping and empty space would make prominent or connected, and choose for the brief. Do this proportionately; do not produce extra variants unless useful or requested. A familiar arrangement or retaining the current layout is valid when it serves the intended communication, not merely because it fits and is readable.

The references below are examples of possible arrangements and their constraints, not a routing table or an exhaustive menu. Some retained specimens author or organize information beyond Kata's supplied-content scope; they do not authorize that work. Use their compositional ideas where relevant without inventing criteria or priorities. A comparison does not automatically require a matrix, and unequal importance does not automatically require golden proportions.

| Reference example | Supplied relationship or priority | Read |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Aligned comparison | Alternatives are already presented on common criteria | [comparison](references/comparison.md) |
| Whole and parts | Parts and the whole are already identified | [composition](references/composition.md) |
| Nested groups | Membership is already specified | [hierarchy](references/hierarchy.md) |
| Directed dependencies | The entities and edge meaning are already specified | [relationship](references/relationship.md) |
| Time series | A consistently defined measure is supplied at ordered times | [change over time](references/change-over-time.md) |
| Asymmetric composition | The brief supports one item receiving more space beside its context | [golden cut](references/golden-cut.md) |

Read [shared layout assessment](references/layout-assessment.md) for checks that apply across forms, including when to keep the current arrangement. Then read the relevant form reference for its particular constraints. If none fits, compose from the supplied intent and relationships rather than forcing a catalogue match. Explain the consequential choice and trade-off briefly. Do not silently shorten text, remove facts, or redesign the content. Preserve any association between content and its caption, source, unit, or qualification.

## Responsibility and output

Kata's current responsibility is the arrangement of supplied content: placement, available space, grouping, and reading order. It does not own content authorship or internal information modeling. The renderer owns output behavior and format; an optional brand skill owns visual identity. See [integration](references/integration.md). For Lys, use the available Lys authoring guidance. Kata works without Lys or a brand skill and does not install either.

Only claim an integration was tested when the actual output has been inspected. Review the rendered result in its intended contexts; structural validity alone does not establish readability. Keep authoring instructions out of the audience-facing content.

The worked examples use fictional data and are specimens, not evidence to reuse in a real presentation. The rationales are design judgments; Gestalt vocabulary supplies analogies, not proof of universal effectiveness. Support specific empirical psychology claims with relevant research and describe its limits.
# Aligned comparison

**Scope:** This reference preserves the existing form and worked specimen.
Use it to arrange supplied comparisons only when common criteria are already
provided. Structure notes describe the specimen's constraints; they do not
authorize deriving criteria, rewriting material, or splitting or hiding content
without the content author's direction. For shared checks, see
[layout assessment](layout-assessment.md).

**Form:** Several alternatives share the same attributes. **Audience question:** Which trade-offs matter for this decision?

An existing matrix accommodates exact values or mixed units; aligned bars or dots can accommodate content centered on one numeric measure. A matrix may be hard to read with long descriptions or unrelated criteria. If supplied material does not fit legibly, state the constraint; split or filter it only when the content author directs that change.

## Structure

In the supplied specimen, alternatives occupy columns and criteria rows, with units in row labels. Identical attributes share positions and precision is consistent. A supplied priority may appear earlier in reading order. Recommendation styling belongs only when it is already supported by the supplied content and brief. Preserve source representations of missing data; do not reinterpret them.

## Retained worked example (historical)

Fictional service launch. An operations lead needs a launch route within six weeks and an initial budget of €80k. Costs are one-time estimates; lead times are estimates too.

| Criterion | Build | Buy | Partner |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Lead time, weeks | 12 | 4 | 6 |
| Initial cost, €k | 120 | 60 | 80 |
| Control | Full | Limited | Shared |

**Before:** The historical example contrasts three paragraphs, each listing its option's time, cost, and control.

**After headline (historical):** “Buy and Partner meet both launch limits”

**After layout:** The historical specimen shows one matrix with constraints above it. It emphasizes two options and adds a trade-off sentence. These are retained example choices, not instructions to rewrite supplied material or assert a priority absent from a user's brief.

## Rationale and limits

Design judgment: common alignment makes a criterion easy to scan. Similarity is a useful Gestalt analogy for repeated cell treatment; it does not prove this matrix is best for every audience. Avoid unrelated icons, ratings without defined scales, and weighted totals without agreed weights.

**Check:** All nine values survive the transformation, both constraints remain visible, and no option becomes a recommendation by styling alone.
# Directed dependencies

**Scope:** This reference preserves the existing form and worked specimen.
Use it to arrange supplied dependencies only when the entities and edge
meaning are already provided. Structure notes describe the specimen's
constraints; they do not authorize deriving edges, changing their meaning, or
splitting content without the content author's direction. For shared checks,
see [layout assessment](layout-assessment.md).

**Form:** Named entities connected by a defined relation. **Audience question:** What must happen before what?

The retained examples use a small directed diagram for supplied prerequisite relations. Dates, durations, or a dense network may constrain how legibly the supplied content fits. Keep the supplied edge meaning explicit: data flow, ownership, influence, and prerequisite are different relations. Do not convert between them or split the network without the content author's direction.

## Structure

In the prerequisite specimen, prerequisites sit left of dependent work, the arrow meaning is stated, and connector endpoints, forks, and joins remain clear. Horizontal distance does not imply duration. Preserve supplied edge types and meanings.

## Retained worked example (historical)

Fictional service launch. The launch lead needs to explain readiness prerequisites.

- Research must finish before Design.
- Design must finish before Build.
- Build must finish before Launch.
- Compliance must finish before Launch.

**Before:** A list of these four rules.

**After headline (historical):** “Launch requires both Build and Compliance”

**After layout:** The historical specimen arranges the supplied prerequisites left to right, with Compliance on a separate branch. The annotation clarifies edge meaning and that spacing does not represent duration. These are retained example choices, not instructions to infer relations.

## Rationale and limits

Design judgment: continuous paths make the prerequisite chain traceable. Good continuation is a Gestalt analogy, not proof of easier comprehension. The lower branch is a separate prerequisite, not a later phase. No durations or slack are supplied, so the diagram cannot identify a critical path or a bottleneck.

**Check:** All four source edges survive; no connector merely passes through a box that could look like another dependency. A cycle should remain explicit and trigger a question about its meaning, not be silently deleted.
# Assessing a layout

Use these checks with any form, including forms not listed in the catalogue. They concern the arrangement of supplied content; they do not ask you to rewrite it or model relationships within it.

## Understand the supplied emphasis

Read enough of the supplied material to avoid misleading emphasis. Identify what it says, which audience or purpose the brief names, and which context, unit, source, or qualification must remain attached for the message to stay accurate. Use only priorities supported by the brief or material. If the intended priority is unclear and would change the layout, ask. If it would not change the layout, retain the supplied treatment or use equal emphasis.

For example, a supplied figure of **8%**, qualified by “among surveyed respondents,” should retain that qualification beside the figure. Giving 8% a dominant field may make it look more general or certain than the supplied evidence supports.

If two supplied claims have equal priority, enlarging one suggests a ranking that the source does not give. Keep them balanced, or ask which one should lead if the brief requires a clear primary message.

## Check the arrangement

- Does size, placement, grouping, or reading order express only the priority supported by the brief?
- Are captions, labels, units, sources, and qualifications readable and clearly associated with the content they explain?
- Are meaningful scales, grouping, and labels preserved? Does the arrangement work at its intended presentation size and phone size when relevant?
- Does the rendered output show clipping or overflow, or text that has become too small to read?
- Does content remain readable without JavaScript and in print where supported? Does the renderer’s keyboard navigation work?
- What does the arrangement make dominant, connected or separate, and does that serve the audience and intended effect?
- Would a plausible alternative express the intended relationship better, or does the current arrangement have a communicative reason to stay?

Keep the current arrangement when its hierarchy and relationships serve the brief as well as preserving meaning and readability. Do not change it merely to demonstrate novelty. If space is insufficient, state the constraint for the author. Do not shorten prose, remove facts, invent content, or redesign a supplied item to force it into the layout. Record the sizes and modes reviewed, and note any verification gaps.
